  "scope": "Shared CRT-free surface readback mechanics own chunk bounds, request ids, the process response slot, heartbeat/silence waits, status/id/length validation and accepted row prefixes. Readback then transport locking pins the mapping while waiting and prevents another frontend closing it. D3D9 retains API pool/format/ownership guards, valid-shadow fast copies, opaque front alpha and destination validity. Its mandatory flush reports real transport failure rather than ordinary draw-drop continuation. Native production C tests check four chunks with padding, invalid metadata, transport failures, stale/failed/short replies, accepted prefixes, UINT wrap and a 15-second live backlog; actual frontend extraction checks shadow/error/alpha/capacity behavior and strict submit failures. XP public GetRenderTargetData performs two complete 1024x1024 CPU image checks with explicit simulated known-color host replies and first-chunk stale-id/status/size failure injections retaining sentinels. Actual GPU replay independently executes the captured full-quad geometry and all seven exact readback requests; 5227520 pixels per backend match known input. Simulated guest replies are never described as real GPU replies. No original Microsoft HAL reference is claimed. This is the common mechanism prerequisite for D3D8 CopyRects/GetFrontBuffer/RT locking; S2-36 itself and remaining stages 4/5/6 continue.",
